1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,140
Module 2, Video 2.3, The Explanatory Memorandum and Preamble.

2
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:13,940
Right. So draft the case for and the recitals with Claude from your strategic foundation document, and then check every claim against a real statute.

3
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:19,200
So why are the first two components of the decree the ones a non-lawyer can lead on?

4
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:25,400
Well, I mean, they are about the case and the context rather than the binding rules themselves.

5
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:35,360
Yeah. Both start from the strategic foundation document you already have in your ministry, and, you know, both are focused drafting tasks an AI assistant does well with

6
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:36,060
you reviewing.

7
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:41,440
Okay, so what must the explanatory memorandum convey to your minister and cabinet?

8
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:46,080
Uh, it must convey four points. First, the problem the decree solves.

9
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:47,560
Like the issue of fragmented systems.

10
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:51,100
Yes, fragmentation where the citizen is asked the same thing many times.

11
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:53,800
Second, what the decree does in plain language.

12
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:54,320
Keep it clear.

13
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:58,949
Exactly. Third, why now, detailing the cost of another year without a mandate.

14
00:00:59,110 --> 00:01:02,500
And fourth, the expected benefit to citizens and the state.

15
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:04,370
So it's a direct flow from previous work.

16
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:08,940
Yes, it is largely a translation of your strategic foundation document.

17
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:11,620
Now, how does the preamble differ,

18
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:15,140
and, uh, why does it require caution?

19
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,840
Well, the preamble requires caution across three points.

20
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:18,520
One,

21
00:01:19,170 --> 00:01:22,360
the recitals, which are formal findings the decree rests on.

22
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:28,240
Okay. Two, the legal authority, which is the constitutional or statutory power to make this decree.

23
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:29,640
Which is critical to get right.

24
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:38,100
Absolutely. And three, placing a bracketed confirm on every citation until a qualified lawyer verifies the power exists and supports this decree.

25
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:39,400
You can't leave that to chance.

26
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:43,460
No. I mean, a wrong authority citation is the fastest way to have the decree sent back.

27
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:47,840
So what is the balance of work between the AI and, and the official in all this?

28
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:53,460
Uh, the AI is the easy ninety percent, turning the foundation document into a draft in minutes.

29
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:55,520
Leaving the official to handle the details.

30
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:02,760
Yes. You are the essential ten. You confirm every legal citation against a real statute, soften any claim the evidence does not support,

31
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:05,160
and mark the rest with a bracketed confirm.

32
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:07,680
So generate fast, confirm carefully.

33
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:12,860
Exactly. The AI accelerates drafting, but does not get the law right for you.

34
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:14,480
Right. We still carry that responsibility.

35
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:17,000
You do. The first two components come quickly.

36
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:20,720
Lead on the case, generate the drafts, and confirm the law.

37
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:24,220
The hardest component is the draft articles, the binding rules themselves.

38
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:25,489
Sources are in the video description.
